! b2-doors-cells — T9 (process-global doors) and T10 (boot cells)
Reader-Two: Lares (Tasked Spirit). Read 2026-10-31 at main 27bc0cb6b. Read in full: worker-handle.ts (231 lines); open-node-vessel.ts 709-1090, 1190-1230, 1340-1420, 1500-1556, 1685-2060, 2480-2625, 3010-3110, 3245-3450, 3725-3770, plus targeted greps over the rest of 709-3546 for every cell. I did not read lines 1090-1190, 1230-1340, 1420-1500, 1556-1685, 2060-2480, 2625-3010 line by line; I located every cell set/read in them by grep and read the hits in context. Treat cell tables below as grep-complete, narrative-complete only for the read spans.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/findings`>>

!! FINDINGS (separate from cut notes)
F1. LATENT CROSS-VESSEL LEAK (fails open on overlap, not reachable today). The four doors are module `let`s in worker-handle.ts. Two vessels with overlapping lifetimes in one process: the second boot silently overwrites the first's door; the daemon island of vessel A then answers through vessel B's store, cas dir and, worst, custody write (`setCustodyWriteDoor` at open-node-vessel.ts ~2488 seals the island's veil archive export through `sovereign.writeVeilArchive(bytes, personaGroupId)` of whichever vessel set it last). A floor (non-sovereign) vessel never sets the custody door, so it INHERITS a live sovereign neighbour's door: its island's archive export would seal under the neighbour's key and face. Teardown is unconditional (`setCasDoor(null)` etc.), so A closing nulls B's live doors (that direction fails closed: rest `rested:false`, cas miss, dyad `aside`, custody `written:false`). No production path boots two vessels in one process (main.ts opens one) and no test does today (every `openNodeVessel(` test file calls it once; the dyad/rest/custody tests use one process-global door at a time). So: not exploitable now; it is the standing reason T9 exists and it breaks "one owner per registry" and "no key on a surface" the moment a host runs two vessels.
F2. ORDER-DEPENDENT FAIL-OPEN WINDOW, by comment only. `kahuProtected` stays null until `systemCanon` (open-node-vessel.ts ~1986-1991, inside the keel, before the daemon). While null, `protectedDocs.ids()` returns the empty map, `incomplete()` false, so the guard withholds NOTHING from a wire peer. The code's claim ("stands in systemCanon, before any wire peer can") holds only because the sync gates stand from ONE call site: `standSyncGates` runs at afterDaemon (~3272) or via `resweepGates`, which stays null until that line. Nothing in code enforces it. Same shape, documented fail-open nulls: `antigenRing` null "denies nobody" (set ~1198, before keel), `edgeShadows` null "sets nobody aside" (set ~1416). All three are set in straight-line code before keel and before any gate; none is reachable by a wire peer today. T10 must not let this window widen; see T10 verdict.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/t9-instance-owned-doors`>>

!! T9 — instance-owned doors
# Span: packages/lararium-node/src/worker-handle.ts, whole file (231 lines). Four module lets: `casDoor` (l.15), `restDoor` (l.48), `dyadDoor` (l.68), `custodyWriteDoor` (l.97). Setters `setCasDoor` 18, `setRestDoor` 51, `setDyadDoor` 70, `setCustodyWriteDoor` 99. Getters: `fetchThroughCasDoor` 21 (exported, used outside), `answerDocRest` 54, `answerDyadAsk` 73, `answerCustodyWrite` 102 (all exported so tests ask without a worker). Attachers `attachCasDoor/RestDoor/DyadDoor/CustodyWriteDoor` (module-private) read the global at message time. `nodeWorkerHandle(w,{island})` picks which attachers a worker gets by IslandClass (daemon: all four; wiki: cas + `attachRefusals`; session: none).
# Does: gives each worker_threads Worker the vessel-side answers to its door asks (cas:want, doc:rest, dyad:ask, custody:write), reading whichever door the PROCESS holds now.
# State: four module globals; no env, fs or clock. `attachRefusals` keeps a per-worker `logged` Set.
# Callers (workspace grep, src+tests+tools; `automerge-repo/` submodule irrelevant):
#* setters, src: open-node-vessel.ts l.105 import; `setRestDoor` 1083 (down 1084), `setCasDoor` 1609 (down 1816; and a second unconditional `setCasDoor(null)` in the Herm `dispose` ~3740), `setDyadDoor` 2092 (down 2100), `setCustodyWriteDoor` 2488 only `if (sovereign)` (down 2490).
#* getter outside worker-handle: vessel-island-pool.ts:25/39 `nodeContentReader` calls `fetchThroughCasDoor` (the session island's content read; a fifth reader path the cut must thread).
#* spawn seam: open-daemon-vm.ts:37/148 `spawnWorker: (url)=>nodeSpawnDaemonWorker(url)`; vessel-island-pool.ts:25/93 `nodeSpawnSessionWorker/WikiWorker`.
#* tools: tools/capability-inventory.mjs 310-313 (sets all four with counting doors) and 340 (clears them), plus a scan-name list at 114-116 that includes `answerBrowserDyadAsk`, a name that exists nowhere in src (only a negative assertion in dyad-door.test.ts:161); tools/capability-inventory.json (table of call sites, 11 `worker-handle.ts: Worker` rows).
#* browser twin: NO doors. packages/lararium-browser/src/worker-handle.ts has none; browser dyad/cas reads ride holder-routes.ts ("cas:want": "owner") and `resolveByCid` over OPFS. So T9 is node-only today; the browser twin has nothing to burn.
# Tests that reach them (each sets the global, resets it in afterEach):
#* island-doors-by-class.test.ts (cas/custody/rest per class via fake Worker, plus a source scan that only open-daemon-vm.ts spawns daemon workers, vessel-island-pool.ts the others, and no file calls `nodeWorkerHandle` itself).
#* dyad-door.test.ts (answerDyadAsk table, per-class, plus a SOURCE-TEXT scan of open-node-vessel.ts: exactly one `setDyadDoor(async (fleetRoot, deviceKey) => {` and `down: () => setDyadDoor(null)`; the cut will red these on purpose and must rewrite them in the same commit).
#* rest-door.test.ts (answerDocRest, with a heads case at l.208), custody-write-door.test.ts (carrier "veil" only; "archive" refused), surface-mount-vessel-path.test.ts (sets cas, rest, custody together around a mount).
#* No test reaches the door wiring in open-node-vessel.ts except through a whole boot (8 test files import openNodeVessel/Herm; none asserts door standing/dropping except the dyad source scan).
# Lift target: substrate adapter, node side. A capability-named record `VesselDoors { fetch: CasDoor|null; rest: RestDoor|null; dyad: DyadDoor|null; custodyWrite: CustodyWriteDoor|null; (u1) sessionSign: SessionSignDoor|null }` built by the composer, handed to `nodeSpawnDaemonWorker/WikiWorker/SessionWorker/nodeWorkerHandle` and `nodeContentReader`. Ports are named by capability (fetch-by-cid, rest-in-store, edge-standing, custody-seal, session-sign), not by host. Mutable record fields (stood, then nulled at teardown) keep today's "stand later, drop at close" timing, since the daemon worker spawns BEFORE some doors stand (cas at 1609 precedes the spawn; custody/dyad at 2092/2488 precede `openDaemonVm` at 2579, but the rest door at 1083 precedes all). The record object must exist at composition start and each `stood.push(down)` clears only its own field of its own record.
# Laws: one owner per registry (cured: one record per vessel); capability not host (ports named by duty); no key on a surface (custody door stays daemon-class only: `attachRefusals` and the class gate must move with the record, unchanged); old shapes burn (the four `set*` exports and `fetchThroughCasDoor` global burn, no shim); web3-only untouched; no global now untouched.
# Risk:
#* u1 premise check: the task said u1 adds the fifth door at worker-handle.ts ~123-155 as uncommitted. Measured: the working diff in ~/.lares-scratch/u1/wt is small (8 files, `SessionSignBook.hold` first-booking-wins, holder `owedActs` becomes a thunk, one json row). worker-handle.ts's fifth door lives in u1's COMMITTED head (cac93c802, over e0a7d18d3): lines 123-155 `SessionSignDoor` type, `sessionSignDoor` global, `setSessionSignDoor`, `answerSessionSign`, `attachSessionSignDoor`, a `session:sign` branch in `attachRefusals` (wiki island refuses by name), `attachSessionSignDoor` in the daemon branch. open-node-vessel.ts in u1: import +1, `sessionSigns = new SessionSignBook()` and `setSessionSignDoor(sessionSigns.door(()=>dialAnchor.chain()))` at ~1698-1701 with `down: setSessionSignDoor(null)`, new field `sessionSigns` on NodeBootPrep/NodeVesselResult/compose (3 more hunks). u1 also touches uds-channel.ts, uds-session-door.ts, node-daemon-island.ts, main.ts, mesh island-protocol.ts, keyhive. Git diff vs 27bc0cb6b: 21 files, +797/-87. T9 must start from u1's landed worker-handle.ts, and the record gets 5 fields. The fifth door is the same shape, so it adds a row to every test table above and to capability-inventory.mjs/json.
#* ralias (diff vs 27bc0cb6b: open-node-vessel.ts +72) touches the boot-cell region too: new `reverdictLater` noop-default let (~725), `protectedMemo`/`boundWaiter` (~846-910), `faceGrantRing` (~1002), the `bootstrapTiddlers` read (~1894-1943), plus vessel-residency-wiring.ts (+12). No overlap with the door call sites, but it overlaps T10 heavily (see T10).
#* Order: `setCasDoor` stands at ~1609, but the Herm `dispose` calls it again (double null; harmless, burns with the record).
#* Open vs closed flip: today no door = every ask answers closed (miss, `rested:false`, `aside`, `written:false`). The record must keep that default per field, and a worker spawned with no record must behave the same. A record handed to a worker by reference keeps late-stand semantics; one handed by value would freeze nulls (the easy wrong cut; the red below catches it).
#* capability-inventory.mjs measures the node island table by setting the four globals on a dist module: it must switch to building a record and passing it to the spawn functions, and its json table regenerates (u1's json diff already edits one row; expect a rebase fight on tools/capability-inventory.json).
# Red-first: (a) two records, two workers in one process: worker A's `doc:rest` ask reaches A's store door only, B's reaches B's, and distinct `cas:want` cids answer distinct bytes (the red on today's globals: the second `set` overwrites the first). (b) Control: one record, one worker, the old single-door behaviour answers identically. (c) Teardown isolation: nulling record A's fields leaves B's door answering. (d) Floor control: a vessel record built with no custody door never answers `written:true` even while a sibling record holds one (the F1 inheritance case). (e) Class gate carried: wiki-class worker given a full record still refuses custody/rest/dyad/session-sign by name. (f) late-stand: a door set on the record AFTER the worker spawned answers (catches the by-value cut). Rewrite dyad-door.test.ts's source-text scan to the new wiring site in the same commit.
# VERDICT: CUT-READY after u1 lands (sequence it behind u1's worker-handle.ts, as the road already says). Fork to settle at brief time, lean given: the record is built by `prepareNodeBootOn` and passed down through `openDaemonVm` options and the pool constructor (lean: yes, explicit threading, no ambient default); do NOT keep a module-level "current record" fallback, that would be the shim the laws burn.

